{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "am-02-mean-price-missing-price",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Finding Mean Price and Missing Ingredient Price",
    "description": "Use weighted averages in both directions to find mixture price, missing component price, and combined group values.",
    "teachingApproach": "Begin by rebuilding mean price from quantity-weighted total cost. Then reverse the same equation to find a missing ingredient price. Progress to fixed mixing ratios, treating two equal varieties as one combined component, and weighted profit rates. Every shortcut is checked against the underlying total-value equation.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 29,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 21, Alligation or Mixture. Approved scope: Teach the weighted-average form of mean price when quantities are known, then reverse it to find an unknown ingredient price. Cover equal quantities, unequal ratios, a missing dearer or cheaper price, and a third variety combined with two known varieties in a fixed ratio. Include the chapter pattern where two first varieties are mixed equally and treated as one combined component before alligation with a third variety. Also cover average profit percentage across two parts as another weighted-average problem. Learners should keep quantity weights visible and verify that the resulting mean lies between the component values whenever all components are positive."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's run mixture prices in both directions.\n\nIf 4 kg costs ₹15/kg and 6 kg costs ₹25/kg, the final price is not the simple middle. Quantity decides the weight. Once that makes sense, we can also work backwards to find a missing ingredient price.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A mixture price is a weighted average, not always the middle of the two prices.\n\nMix 2 kg at ₹10 with 3 kg at ₹20.\nTotal cost = 20 + 60 = ₹80.\nTotal quantity = 5 kg.\nMean = ₹16/kg.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The weights are the quantities.\n\nA useful formula is:\nmean = (q₁p₁ + q₂p₂) ÷ (q₁+q₂).\n\nUse it when the quantities are already known. Alligation is faster when the mean is known and the ratio is missing.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "4 kg at ₹15/kg is mixed with 6 kg at ₹25/kg. What is the mean price?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "input": {
        "type": "CHOICE",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "label": "₹19/kg"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "label": "₹21/kg"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "label": "₹20/kg"
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "label": "₹40/kg"
          }
        ]
      },
      "questionReference": {
        "mode": "INLINE",
